\subsection{Verifiable Searchable Encryption and Attribute-Based Search}

Verifiable searchable encryption (VSE) enables users to
verify the correctness and integrity of outsourced search
results, while attribute-based searchable encryption (ABSE)
provides fine-grained access control. Early
ABSE schemes~\cite{ref6} introduced attribute-constrained
search, which was later extended with hierarchical
search~\cite{ref36}, hidden access
policies~\cite{ref37}, multi-keyword
search~\cite{ref5,ref38,ref7},
blockchain-assisted auditability~\cite{ref39,ref40},
revocation~\cite{ref41,ref42}, and resistance to
keyword-guessing and leakage
attacks~\cite{ref43,ref44,ref45}. Recent VSE
schemes~\cite{ref13,ref14,ref15,ref16,ref17,ref35}
further support authenticated query processing through
result verification, secure delegation, provenance
validation, and blockchain-assisted integrity assurance.
Dynamic searchable encryption enhances multi-query
search~\cite{ref8}, forward/backward
privacy~\cite{ref46,ref47}, update
privacy~\cite{ref19,ref20}, and
privacy-preserving trapdoor
generation~\cite{ref18,ref19}. Nevertheless, existing
schemes remain predominantly keyword-oriented and do not
jointly support authenticated conjunctive numerical range
search with privacy-preserving encrypted aggregation.

\subsection{Privacy-Preserving Range Query}

Privacy-preserving range-query processing has been widely
studied for encrypted databases and IoT systems.
Order-preserving and order-revealing
encryption~\cite{ref48} enables efficient ciphertext
comparison but inherently leaks ordering information.
Encrypted range-query
schemes~\cite{ref21,ref20,ref22} improve retrieval for
numerical and spatial data but provide limited support for
conjunctive multidimensional queries and encrypted
analytics, while learned-index
approaches~\cite{ref15} mainly optimize indexing
efficiency rather than integrating access control,
aggregation, or authenticated verification.

Recent studies improve the scalability of encrypted range
search. Volume-Hiding
SSE~\cite{ref23} reduces retrieval leakage, Revocable
DSSE~\cite{ref24} supports dynamic healthcare search,
Scheme~\cite{ref25} enables efficient multidimensional
range queries, and Trinity~\cite{ref26} introduces
forward-secure spatio-temporal search using SHVE and
quotient filters. Although these schemes improve search
efficiency and expressiveness, they treat retrieval as an
independent task without integrated encrypted aggregation
or authenticated result verification.

More recent efforts combine range queries with
attribute-based and post-quantum searchable encryption.
ABSE-Range~\cite{ref27} supports attribute-authorized
numerical range queries but incurs increasing trapdoor
complexity as the query range expands. Lattice-based
searchable encryption~\cite{ref28} provides post-quantum
security under LWE assumptions but incurs substantial
lattice-computation overhead, limiting practicality for
resource-constrained IIoT deployments.

\subsection{Searchable Encryption with Privacy-Preserving Aggregation}

Homomorphic encryption enables computation over encrypted data and
has increasingly been combined with searchable encryption for secure
cloud analytics. The verifiable searchable encryption
framework~\cite{ref13} integrates partial homomorphic encryption,
B$+$-Tree indexing, bitmap filtering, and blockchain-assisted
verification for Boolean search. SEaaS~\cite{ref34} combines
searchable encryption with homomorphic analytics, while
Refs.~\cite{ref18,ref19,ref20} improve query privacy, update privacy,
and multi-user search.

Existing schemes, however, typically optimize search, aggregation,
or verification separately. Encrypted aggregation is often treated
as post-processing, adding computation and communication overhead,
while verification mechanisms mainly ensure retrieval correctness
without jointly providing aggregation integrity, authenticated
completeness, freshness, and rollback resistance.

Table~\ref{tab:feature_comparison} compares representative schemes.
Verifiable searchable encryption~\cite{ref13,ref16,ref17} supports
authenticated search, but Refs.~\cite{ref13,ref16} lack numerical
range queries and Ref.~\cite{ref17} lacks encrypted aggregation.
Range-query schemes~\cite{ref23,ref24,ref25,ref26,ref27,ref28}
improve retrieval efficiency but generally separate search,
aggregation, and verification. SEaaS~\cite{ref34} supports encrypted
aggregation but lacks conjunctive range search, fine-grained access
control, and verifiable query processing.

In contrast, BVCRSA integrates authenticated hierarchical range-cover
indexing, protected bitmap filtering, encrypted aggregation, and
blockchain-assisted query-state verification within a unified query
workflow. It thereby supports conjunctive range search, fine-grained
access control, privacy-preserving aggregation, and authenticated
query-state integrity, commitment-relative completeness, freshness,
and rollback resistance for outsourced IIoT analytics.
\section{Preliminaries}

\subsection*{ABSE Instantiation}

BVCRSA instantiates attribute-based searchable encryption
(ABSE) over bilinear groups
$(\mathbb G_1,\mathbb G_2,\mathbb G_T,q,g_1,g_2,e)$ using
BN128. Its interface is
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{ABSE}=
(\mathsf{Setup},\mathsf{KeyGen},\mathsf{Index},
\mathsf{Token},\mathsf{Test},\mathsf{Enc},\mathsf{Dec}).
\label{eq:abse-interface}
\end{equation}
$\mathsf{Index}$, $\mathsf{Token}$, and $\mathsf{Test}$
provide policy-controlled keyword matching, whereas
$\mathsf{Enc}$ and $\mathsf{Dec}$ protect node and record
keys under an LSSS access policy
$\mathcal P=(M,\rho)$.

For keyword $\tau$, policy $\mathcal P$, and context
$(e,qid)$, correctness requires
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{ABSE.Test}(PP,I_\tau,Tok_{\tau'},e,qid)=1
\Rightarrow
\tau=\tau'\land\mathcal A\models\mathcal P,
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{ABSE.Dec}(SK_{\mathcal A},C_K,e)=K
\iff
\mathcal A\models\mathcal P.
\end{equation}
BVCRSA assumes adaptive keyword privacy, randomized
context-bound tokens, encapsulated-key confidentiality,
policy enforcement, and user-collusion resistance under
the adopted ABSE construction.

\paragraph*{Security Assumptions.}
BVCRSA assumes that the instantiated ABSE provides search
correctness and soundness, encapsulated-key confidentiality,
and user-collusion resistance under the underlying bilinear-group
and hash/PRF assumptions. These properties are invoked explicitly
in Theorems~4, 6, and~7.

\section{Our Proposed Scheme}
\subsection{System Model}
\label{subsec:system_model}

BVCRSA comprises seven entities, as shown in
Fig.~\ref{fig:system_model}.

\begin{enumerate}

\item \textbf{IIoT Sensor Nodes.}
Collect measurements, derive canonical-node metadata, encrypt records
and aggregation values, and authenticate packets sent to the gateway.

\item \textbf{Edge Gateway.}
The honest-but-curious, non-colluding gateway authenticates packets,
assigns bitmap positions, constructs policy-bound indexes and protected
bitmaps, registers aggregation entries, and anchors signed epoch
commitments. It observes index metadata but cannot decrypt records
or aggregates.

\item \textbf{Cloud Server.}
The potentially malicious cloud stores outsourced ciphertexts and
indexes, evaluates ABSE tokens, returns authenticated query data, and
performs homomorphic aggregation. Invalid responses are detected by
commitment and client-side verification.

\item \textbf{Blockchain Network.}
The permissioned blockchain stores signed epoch commitments,
providing authenticated state, finality, freshness, and rollback
resistance; records and indexes remain off-chain.

\item \textbf{Data User.}
An authorized user generates query tokens, verifies proofs,
reconstructs authorized bitmaps, evaluates conjunctive results, and
verifies aggregates before threshold decryption or record retrieval.

\item \textbf{Threshold Decryption Authorities (TDAs).}
Each of the $n$ TDAs holds a Threshold EC-ElGamal share; at least
$t$ provide verifiable partial decryptions for authorized aggregate
requests.

\item \textbf{Trusted Authority (TA).}
The TA initializes system parameters, issues credentials and keys,
and distributes threshold shares, but does not participate after
setup.

\end{enumerate}

