\subsection{Threat Model}

We consider PPT adversaries seeking to infer sensor or query
information, violate access policies, recover aggregate plaintexts,
or omit, modify, fabricate, or replay query responses.

\subsubsection{Honest-but-Curious Edge Gateway}

The Edge Gateway faithfully authenticates sensor packets, constructs
the searchable index and protected bitmaps, registers aggregation
metadata, and anchors signed epoch commitments. Its authorized view
includes sensor identities, update times, searchable dimensions,
canonical-node memberships, and bitmap positions.

The gateway exclusively holds $K_{\mathrm{sel}}$ and is therefore
trusted for correct node-key derivation, index construction, and
bitmap formation. It holds neither the ABSE master secret, user or
record keys, nor any Threshold EC-ElGamal share and thus cannot
decrypt records or aggregates. The gateway is assumed
honest-but-curious and non-colluding with the cloud. A malicious
gateway may fabricate or omit index/bitmap state before commitment;
such behavior and gateway--cloud collusion are outside the threat
model. Hence, integrity and completeness are guaranteed only
relative to the gateway-committed state.

\subsubsection{Malicious Cloud Server}

The cloud is privacy-curious and malicious during query execution.
It observes encrypted records, ABSE tokens, protected bitmaps,
aggregation metadata, access patterns, and
$\mathcal L_{\mathrm{BVCRSA}}$, and may omit, modify, substitute, or
replay query results. Signed blockchain commitments, Merkle proofs,
aggregation multi-proofs, query binding, and independent homomorphic
recomputation detect such deviations. Under the stated cryptographic
assumptions, the cloud cannot decrypt records or aggregates, generate
unauthorized tokens, or forge authenticated query states except with
negligible probability. Availability against a nonresponsive cloud
is outside the scope.

\subsubsection{Unauthorized and Colluding Users}

Unauthorized users may attempt to forge or replay tokens, recover
unauthorized keys, or collude with other users or the cloud.
Collusion-resistant ABSE prevents independently issued credentials
from jointly satisfying a policy that none satisfies individually.
Tokens are bound to the finalized epoch, query identifier, and
expiration time to prevent cross-query and stale replay.

\subsubsection{Threshold Decryption Authorities}

Each of the $n$ TDAs holds one Threshold EC-ElGamal share. Corruption
of fewer than $t$ TDAs, including collusion with users or the cloud,
does not reveal the decryption key or aggregate plaintexts, while
DLEQ proofs detect invalid partial decryptions. Corruption of at
least $t$ TDAs and TDA refusal attacks are outside the scope.

\subsubsection{Blockchain and Deployment Assumptions}

The permissioned blockchain provides authenticated access, consensus
finality, and immutable finalized states. Users verify the referenced
epoch, gateway signature, timestamp, and validity period; modifying
or rolling back an accepted state therefore requires violating
blockchain finality, signature security, or hash collision resistance.
Blockchain anchoring authenticates only the gateway-committed state
and does not establish source-level correctness or completeness.

The TA is trusted only for initialization, credential issuance, and
threshold-share distribution. We assume secure cryptographic
primitives, authenticated channels, and nonce uniqueness.
Compromised sensors or TA, malicious gateways, gateway--cloud
collusion, corruption of at least $t$ TDAs, finalized-ledger
rewriting, and physical, side-channel, or denial-of-service attacks
are outside the scope.

