\subsubsection*{\textbf{Phase 1 --- System Setup}}

The Trusted Authority (TA) initializes the system parameters and
distributes the required credentials. Let $\lambda$ be the security
parameter, $\mathcal{U}$ the attribute universe, and
$\mathcal{D}=\{D_1,\ldots,D_d\}$ the searchable numerical dimensions.

\paragraph*{Step 1: ABSE Initialization}

The TA initializes the ABSE construction defined in
Section~III:
\begin{equation}
(PP,MSK)\leftarrow
\mathsf{ABSE.Setup}(1^\lambda).
\label{eq:p1-abse-setup}
\end{equation}
For each authorized user with attribute set
$\mathcal A\subseteq\mathcal U$, it issues
\begin{equation}
SK_{\mathcal A}\leftarrow
\mathsf{ABSE.KeyGen}(MSK,\mathcal A).
\label{eq:p1-abse-keygen}
\end{equation}
BVCRSA subsequently uses the ABSE interface for
policy-controlled canonical-node matching and encapsulation
of node and record keys, with the corresponding query or
record epoch providing context binding.
\paragraph*{Step 2: Threshold Homomorphic Encryption Initialization}

The TA initializes lifted threshold EC-ElGamal over NIST P-256. Let
$G$ generate a group of order $q_E$. It samples
\begin{equation}
x\xleftarrow{\$}\mathbb{Z}_{q_E}^{*},
\qquad
pk_{\mathrm{AHE}}=xG,
\label{eq:ahe-keygen}
\end{equation}
and constructs the degree-$(t-1)$ Shamir polynomial
\begin{equation}
f(z)
=
x+\sum_{j=1}^{t-1}a_jz^j,
\qquad
a_j\xleftarrow{\$}\mathbb{Z}_{q_E}.
\label{eq:shamir-polynomial}
\end{equation}
The secret and public shares of TDA $\ell$ are
\begin{equation}
sk_\ell=f(\ell),
\qquad
pk_\ell=sk_\ell G,
\qquad
\ell\in\{1,\ldots,n\}.
\label{eq:tda-shares}
\end{equation}
A bounded value $v$ is encrypted as
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{Enc}^{\mathrm{AHE}}_{pk_{\mathrm{AHE}}}(v)
=
(rG,vG+rpk_{\mathrm{AHE}}),
\qquad
r\xleftarrow{\$}\mathbb{Z}_{q_E}^{*},
\label{eq:ahe-encryption}
\end{equation}
thereby supporting additive homomorphic aggregation.

\paragraph*{Step 3: Primitive Initialization}

The TA instantiates HMAC-SHA-256 as the pseudorandom function $F$,
a BN254-compatible hash-to-group function $H_g$, SHA-256 as $H$,
HKDF-SHA-256 as $\mathsf{KDF}$, and HKDF-Expand as
$\mathsf{PRG}$. It also instantiates ECDSA over secp256k1 as
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{DS}
=
(\mathsf{KeyGen},\mathsf{Sign},\mathsf{Verify})
\label{eq:signature-scheme}
\end{equation}
and a Chaum--Pedersen DLEQ proof system for verifying threshold
partial decryptions.

\paragraph*{Step 4: Credential Distribution}

For each sensor $i$, the TA derives
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
K_{\mathrm{AES}}^{(i)}
&=
\mathsf{KDF}
(K_{\mathrm{master}},ID_i\parallel\mathtt{enc}),\
K_{\mathrm{HMAC}}^{(i)}
&=
\mathsf{KDF}
(K_{\mathrm{master}},ID_i\parallel\mathtt{hmac}).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:sensor-keys}
\end{equation}
It independently generates the gateway secrets:
\begin{equation}
K_{\mathrm{sel}}
\xleftarrow{$}
{0,1}^{\lambda},
\qquad
(pk_{\mathrm{DO}},sk_{\mathrm{DO}})
\leftarrow
\mathsf{DS.KeyGen}(1^\lambda).
\label{eq:gateway-keys}
\end{equation}
Through authenticated secure channels, the TA distributes
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\text{Sensor }i &:
(K_{\mathrm{AES}}^{(i)},K_{\mathrm{HMAC}}^{(i)},pk_{\mathrm{AHE}}),\
\text{Authorized user} &:
SK_{\mathcal A},\
\text{Edge Gateway} &:
(K_{\mathrm{sel}},sk_{\mathrm{DO}},
{K_{\mathrm{HMAC}}^{(i)}}*i),\
\text{TDA }\ell &:
sk*\ell.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:credential-distribution}
\end{equation}
The gateway uses $K_{\mathrm{HMAC}}^{(i)}$ only to authenticate packets received from sensor $i$ and does not receive $K_{\mathrm{AES}}^{(i)}$. In particular, $K_{\mathrm{sel}}$ is never distributed to users; they obtain only policy-authorized node keys through ABSE.


\paragraph*{Step 5: Public-State Initialization}

The TA publishes
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{Pub}
=
\left(
PP,pk_{\mathrm{AHE}},pk_{\mathrm{DO}},
\{pk_\ell\}_{\ell=1}^{n}
\right)
\label{eq:public-parameters}
\end{equation}
and anchors the initial commitment $Root_0$ on the permissioned
blockchain. The nonpublic state is partitioned as
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathsf{Sec}_{\mathrm{TA}}
&=(MSK,K_{\mathrm{master}}),\\
\mathsf{Sec}_{\mathrm{GW}}
&=(K_{\mathrm{sel}},sk_{\mathrm{DO}}),\\
\mathsf{Sec}_{\mathrm{TDA}}
&=\{sk_\ell\}_{\ell=1}^{n}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:secret-state}
\end{equation}
After setup, the TA does not participate in indexing, query
processing, aggregation, or result verification.

\subsubsection*{\textbf{Phase 2 --- Sensor-Side Encryption and Authenticated Index Construction}}

This phase converts sensor readings into incrementally maintained
authenticated structures for policy-controlled conjunctive search,
encrypted aggregation, and blockchain-verifiable integrity.

\paragraph*{Step 1: Sensor Encryption and Authentication}

For each record
\begin{equation}
D_i=(m_i,\mathbf{x}_i,t_i,v_i),
\qquad
\mathbf{x}_i=(x_{i,1},\ldots,x_{i,d}),
\label{eq:p2-record}
\end{equation}
the sensor derives
\begin{equation}
rid_i=H(ID_i\parallel seq_i),
\qquad
Meta_i=\{(D_j,u_{i,j})\}_{j=1}^{d},
\label{eq:p2-metadata}
\end{equation}
where $u_{i,j}$ is the canonical leaf containing $x_{i,j}$.
For ingestion epoch $e_i$, let
\begin{equation}
Hdr_i=(ID_i,rid_i,seq_i,N_i,e_i).
\label{eq:p2-record-header}
\end{equation}
The sensor derives and encapsulates a record-specific key:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
K_i^{\mathrm{rec}}
&=\mathsf{KDF}(K_{\mathrm{AES}}^{(i)},rid_i\parallel N_i),\\
C_i^{\mathrm{rec}}
&=\mathsf{ABSE.Enc}
(PP,K_i^{\mathrm{rec}},\mathcal P_i,e_i),
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:p2-record-key}
\end{equation}
and computes
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
CT_{\mathrm{AES}}^{(i)}
&=\mathsf{AES\mbox{-}GCM.Enc}_{K_i^{\mathrm{rec}}}
(N_i,D_i;\mathsf{enc}(Hdr_i)),\\
CT_v^{(i)}
&=(r_iG,v_iG+r_i pk_{\mathrm{AHE}}),
\qquad r_i\xleftarrow{\$}\mathbb Z_{q_E}^{*}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:p2-record-encryption}
\end{equation}
It transmits
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal P_i
&=(Hdr_i,CT_{\mathrm{AES}}^{(i)},C_i^{\mathrm{rec}},
CT_v^{(i)},Meta_i,Tag_i),\\
Tag_i
&=\mathsf{HMAC}_{K_{\mathrm{HMAC}}^{(i)}}
(\mathsf{enc}(\mathcal P_i\setminus\{Tag_i\})).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:p2-sensor-packet}
\end{equation}

\paragraph*{Step 2: Edge Verification and Bitmap Update}

The gateway accepts $\mathcal P_i$ only if
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{HMAC.Verify}=1
\land seq_i>seq_i^{\mathrm{last}}
\land\mathsf{Valid}(Meta_i)=1.
\label{eq:p2-packet-verification}
\end{equation}
It atomically registers $rid_i$, assigns a unique position $pos_i$,
updates $seq_i^{\mathrm{last}}$, and sets
\begin{equation}
B_u[pos_i]\leftarrow1,
\qquad
u\in\mathsf{Path}(u_{i,j}),
\quad(D_j,u_{i,j})\in Meta_i.
\label{eq:p2-bitmap-update}
\end{equation}
A repeated $rid_i$ is rejected.

For an authenticated invalidation of an expired, faulty, or
superseded record, the gateway clears its membership from all
affected canonical-node paths:
\begin{equation}
B_u[pos_i]\leftarrow0,
\qquad
u\in\mathsf{Path}(u_{i,j}).
\label{eq:p2-bitmap-invalidation}
\end{equation}
Only the affected bitmap blocks are subsequently versioned,
reprotected, and recommitted as specified in Step~3. A record update
follows an invalidate-and-append procedure, assigning the corrected
record a fresh $rid_i$ and $pos_i$. Historical ciphertexts may be
retained for auditability but are excluded from subsequent queries
and aggregates; physical deletion and secure erasure are outside the
present scope.

\paragraph*{Step 3: Versioned Policy-Bound Index and Bitmap Construction}

For each canonical node $u$ representing $[l_u,r_u]$, the gateway
derives
\begin{equation}
\tau_u=H_g(D_j\parallel l_u\parallel r_u),
\qquad
K_u=F(K_{\mathrm{sel}},D_j\parallel u),
\label{eq:p2-node-key}
\end{equation}
and constructs
\begin{equation}
I_u=\mathsf{ABSE.Index}(PP,\tau_u,\mathcal P_u,v_u),
\qquad
C_u^K=\mathsf{ABSE.Enc}(PP,K_u,\mathcal P_u,v_u),
\label{eq:p2-policy-index}
\end{equation}
where $v_u$ changes only when the node keyword or policy changes.
Thus, only users satisfying $\mathcal P_u$ can recover $K_u$.

For $n_{e_q}^{\mathrm{blk}}=\lceil N_{e_q}/\beta\rceil$, each bitmap
block is protected as
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\widetilde B_{u,b}
&=B_{u,b}\oplus
\mathsf{PRG}\!\left(F(K_u,b\parallel\nu_{u,b}),|B_{u,b}|\right),\\
\sigma_{u,b}^{\mathrm{bmp}}
&=H(\mathtt{BMP}\parallel D_j\parallel u\parallel b
\parallel\nu_{u,b}\parallel\widetilde B_{u,b}),
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:p2-protected-block}
\end{equation}
where $\nu_{u,b}$ is incremented only for modified blocks. Hence,
unchanged blocks require no re-protection.

The gateway commits the bitmap and node state through
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
Root_u^{\mathrm{bmp}}
&=\mathsf{MerkleRoot}
(\mathsf{Sort}_{b}\{\sigma_{u,b}^{\mathrm{bmp}}\}),\\
\sigma_u^{\mathrm{idx}}
&=H(\mathtt{IDX}\parallel D_j\parallel u\parallel v_u
\parallel I_u\parallel C_u^K\parallel N_{e_q}\parallel\beta
\parallel n_{e_q}^{\mathrm{blk}}\parallel Root_u^{\mathrm{bmp}}).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:p2-index-leaf}
\end{equation}

During search, after verifying the corresponding proofs, an authorized
user reconstructs $B_u$ by
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
K_u&=\mathsf{ABSE.Dec}(SK_{\mathcal A},C_u^K,v_u),\\
B_{u,b}
&=\widetilde B_{u,b}\oplus
\mathsf{PRG}\!\left(F(K_u,b\parallel\nu_{u,b}),|B_{u,b}|\right),
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:p2-bitmap-reconstruction}
\end{equation}
then concatenates the blocks in increasing $b$ order and truncates
to $N_{e_q}$ bits. Thus, ABSE-authorized recovery of $K_u$ directly
controls authenticated bitmap reconstruction.

\paragraph*{Step 4: Aggregation Registration}

For each accepted record, the gateway creates
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
CT_1^{(i)}
&=\mathsf{Enc}^{\mathrm{AHE}}_{pk_{\mathrm{AHE}}}(1),\\
h_i^{\mathrm{rec}}
&=H\bigl(
\mathsf{enc}(Hdr_i)\parallel CT_{\mathrm{AES}}^{(i)}
\parallel C_i^{\mathrm{rec}}
\bigr),\\
\mathcal A_i
&=(pos_i,rid_i,e_i,h_i^{\mathrm{rec}},
CT_v^{(i)},CT_1^{(i)}),\\
\sigma_i^{\mathrm{agg}}
&=H\bigl(
\mathtt{AGG}\parallel e_i\parallel
\mathsf{enc}(\mathcal A_i)
\bigr).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:p2-aggregation-entry}
\end{equation}
Since $e_i$ is immutable, historical aggregation entries remain
unchanged across later epochs; $(pos_i,rid_i)$ prevents duplicate
contributions.

\paragraph*{Step 5: Incremental Epoch Commitment and Outsourcing}

At epoch $e_q$, only modified index leaves, bitmap blocks, affected
Merkle paths, and newly appended aggregation entries are updated.
The gateway obtains
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
Root_{e_q}^{\mathrm{idx}}
&=\mathsf{MerkleRoot}
\left(\mathsf{Sort}_{(D_j,u)}
\{\sigma_u^{\mathrm{idx}}\}\right),\\
Root_{e_q}^{\mathrm{agg}}
&=\mathsf{MerkleRoot}
\left(\mathsf{Sort}_{pos_i}
\{\sigma_i^{\mathrm{agg}}\}\right),
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:p2-epoch-roots}
\end{equation}
and commits the resulting snapshot as
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
Root_{e_q}
&=H\bigl(
\mathtt{EPOCH}\parallel e_q\parallel N_{e_q}
\parallel\beta\parallel n_{e_q}^{\mathrm{blk}}
\parallel Root_{e_q}^{\mathrm{idx}}
\parallel Root_{e_q}^{\mathrm{agg}}
\bigr),\\
\sigma_{\mathrm{DO}}
&=\mathsf{DS.Sign}_{sk_{\mathrm{DO}}}
\left(H(Root_{e_q}\parallel e_q\parallel ts_{e_q})\right).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:p2-epoch-commitment}
\end{equation}
It anchors
\begin{equation}
(e_q,N_{e_q},\beta,n_{e_q}^{\mathrm{blk}},
Root_{e_q},ts_{e_q},\sigma_{\mathrm{DO}})
\label{eq:p2-blockchain-anchor}
\end{equation}
on the blockchain and outsources only newly created or modified
objects to the cloud. Unchanged $I_u$, $C_u^K$, bitmap blocks, and
historical aggregation entries are reused.

Therefore, epoch advancement requires incremental state updates and
Merkle-path recomputation rather than full searchable-index
regeneration. The gateway retains $K_{\mathrm{sel}}$, while users
recover only keys authorized by their ABSE policies.


\subsubsection*{\textbf{Phase 3 --- Conjunctive Range-Query Trapdoor Generation}}

An authorized user with attribute key $SK_{\mathcal A}$ generates an
epoch-bound ABSE trapdoor for a conjunctive range query. The numerical
bounds are protected by ABSE, subject to the declared leakage of the
query structure, matched nodes, and access pattern.

\paragraph*{Step 1: Canonical Range Decomposition}

A $d$-dimensional conjunctive range query is
\begin{equation}
Q
=
\bigwedge_{j=1}^{d}
\bigl(D_j,[a_j,b_j]\bigr).
\label{eq:p3-query}
\end{equation}
For each dimension $D_j$, the user computes the minimal canonical
cover
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal{C}_j
&= \mathsf{Cover}(D_j,[a_j,b_j])
= \{u_{j,1},\ldots,u_{j,\ell_j}\}, \\
\ell_j &= O(\log|D_j|).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:p3-canonical-cover}
\end{equation}
Nodes within $\mathcal{C}_j$ are evaluated disjunctively, whereas
the dimension-level results are evaluated conjunctively.

\paragraph*{Step 2: Epoch-Bound Token Generation}

The user retrieves a finalized epoch $e_q$ and its authenticated root
$Root_{e_q}$ from the blockchain, selects an expiration time
$t_{\mathrm{exp}}$, and derives a fresh query identifier:
\begin{equation}
n_q\xleftarrow{\$}\{0,1\}^{\lambda},
\qquad
qid
=
H(e_q\parallel Root_{e_q}\parallel
t_{\mathrm{exp}}\parallel n_q).
\label{eq:p3-query-id}
\end{equation}
For every $u_{j,i}\in\mathcal{C}_j$ representing interval
$[l_{u_{j,i}},r_{u_{j,i}}]$, the user reconstructs the node keyword
\begin{equation}
\tau_{j,i}
=
H_g\bigl(
D_j\parallel l_{u_{j,i}}\parallel r_{u_{j,i}}
\bigr)
\label{eq:p3-node-keyword}
\end{equation}
and invokes the native randomized ABSE token algorithm:
\begin{equation}
Tok_{j,i}
\leftarrow
\mathsf{ABSE.Token}
\bigl(
PP,SK_{\mathcal A},\tau_{j,i},e_q,qid
\bigr).
\label{eq:p3-search-token}
\end{equation}
The binding to $(e_q,qid)$ prevents cross-query or cross-epoch token
substitution, while $t_{\mathrm{exp}}$ limits replay through its
binding to $qid$.

\paragraph*{Step 3: Trapdoor Construction}

For each dimension $D_j$, the user forms
\begin{equation}
\Gamma_j
=
\{Tok_{j,i}\}_{i=1}^{\ell_j}.
\label{eq:p3-token-group}
\end{equation}
The complete trapdoor is
\begin{equation}
T_Q
=
\left(
e_q,N_{e_q},\beta,n_{e_q}^{\mathrm{blk}},
Root_{e_q},qid,t_{\mathrm{exp}},
\{\Gamma_j\}_{j=1}^{d}
\right),
\label{eq:p3-trapdoor}
\end{equation}
where the public bitmap parameters are authenticated through
$Root_{e_q}$. The trapdoor size remains
\begin{equation}
|T_Q|
=
O\left(
\sum_{j=1}^{d}\ell_j
\right)
=
O\left(
\sum_{j=1}^{d}\log|D_j|
\right),
\label{eq:p3-trapdoor-size}
\end{equation}
because $(e_q,N_{e_q},\beta,n_{e_q}^{\mathrm{blk}},
Root_{e_q},qid,t_{\mathrm{exp}})$ has constant size.

For every token $Tok_{j,i}$, the cloud evaluates
\begin{equation}
b_{u,j,i}
=
\mathsf{ABSE.Test}
(PP,I_u,Tok_{j,i},e_q,qid)
\label{eq:p3-token-evaluation}
\end{equation}
over the candidate searchable indexes and retains each node for which
$b_{u,j,i}=1$. Without an additional privacy-preserving lookup
mechanism, a token may need to be tested against all $N_u$ node
indexes, yielding
\begin{equation}
T_{\mathrm{match}}
=
O\left(
N_u
\sum_{j=1}^{d}\ell_j
\right)
=
O(N_um_c),
\label{eq:p3-matching-complexity}
\end{equation}
where $m_c=\sum_{j=1}^{d}\ell_j$. If the selected ABSE provides an
indexed hidden-lookup procedure, this exhaustive test may be replaced
by that procedure, and its leakage and complexity must be stated
accordingly.

The cloud subsequently returns the matching node-key envelopes,
exactly $n_{e_q}^{\mathrm{blk}}$ protected blocks per matched node,
and their Merkle proofs in Phase~4. Node-key recovery and bitmap
reconstruction are performed only by the authorized user. Thus,
$T_Q$ exposes neither $K_{\mathrm{sel}}$ nor any node key.


\subsubsection*{\textbf{Phase 4 --- Verifiable Conjunctive Range Search and Aggregation}}

Using $T_Q$, the cloud identifies policy-authorized canonical nodes
and returns their authenticated protected bitmap blocks. For every
record position $pos_i$ represented by node $u$, the index construction
enforces the policy-consistency condition
\begin{equation}
pos_i\in B_u
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\mathcal P_u\Rightarrow\mathcal P_i,
\label{eq:p4-policy-consistency}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal P_u\Rightarrow\mathcal P_i$ means that any attribute
set satisfying $\mathcal P_u$ also satisfies $\mathcal P_i$. Thus,
node-level search authorization cannot bypass the corresponding
record-level policy.

\paragraph*{Step 1: Authorized Node Matching}

For each $Tok_{j,i}\in\Gamma_j$, the cloud evaluates
\begin{equation}
b_{u,j,i}
=
\mathsf{ABSE.Test}
(PP,I_u,Tok_{j,i},e_q,qid).
\label{eq:p4-token-test}
\end{equation}
If $b_{u,j,i}=1$, it adds $u$ to $\mathcal M_j$ and binds the
response to the token through
\begin{equation}
\chi_{j,i}
=
H(e_q\parallel qid\parallel\mathsf{enc}(Tok_{j,i})).
\label{eq:p4-token-reference}
\end{equation}
The matched-node set is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal U_Q=\bigcup_{j=1}^{d}\mathcal M_j.
\label{eq:p4-matched-nodes}
\end{equation}

The cloud returns
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal{Rsp}_Q^{(1)}
=
\Bigl(
&e_q,qid,N_{e_q},\beta,n_{e_q}^{\mathrm{blk}},\\
&\{\chi_{j,i},D_j,u,I_u,C_u^K,
Root_u^{\mathrm{bmp}},\pi_u^{\mathrm{idx}},\\
&\quad
\{\widetilde B_{u,b},\nu_{u,b},
\sigma_{u,b}^{\mathrm{bmp}},
\pi_{u,b}^{\mathrm{bmp}}\}_{b=1}^{n_{e_q}^{\mathrm{blk}}}
\}_{u\in\mathcal U_Q}
\Bigr).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:p4-first-response}
\end{equation}

\paragraph*{Step 2: Bitmap Verification and Conjunctive Evaluation}

The user verifies the signed finalized state
$(e_q,N_{e_q},\beta,n_{e_q}^{\mathrm{blk}},
Root_{e_q}^{\mathrm{idx}},Root_{e_q}^{\mathrm{agg}})$
against $Root_{e_q}$ and confirms its binding to the unexpired
trapdoor $T_Q$.

For each $Tok_{j,i}$, exactly one returned node must satisfy
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\chi_{j,i}
&=H(e_q\parallel qid\parallel\mathsf{enc}(Tok_{j,i})),\\
\mathsf{ABSE.Test}(PP,I_u,Tok_{j,i},e_q,qid)&=1,\\
\mathsf{MerkleVerify}
(\sigma_u^{\mathrm{idx}},\pi_u^{\mathrm{idx}},
Root_{e_q}^{\mathrm{idx}})&=1.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:p4-match-verification}
\end{equation}
For every matched node, all authenticated blocks
$b\in\{1,\ldots,n_{e_q}^{\mathrm{blk}}\}$ must be present and valid;
otherwise, the response is rejected.

Following the node-key workflow of Phase~2, the user recovers and
reconstructs
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
K_u
&=\mathsf{ABSE.Dec}(SK_{\mathcal A},C_u^K,v_u),\\
B_{u,b}
&=\widetilde B_{u,b}\oplus
\mathsf{PRG}\!\left(
F(K_u,b\parallel\nu_{u,b}),|B_{u,b}|
\right).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:p4-bitmap-recovery}
\end{equation}
The verified blocks are concatenated in increasing $b$ order and
truncated to $N_{e_q}$ bits to obtain $B_u$. The conjunctive result is
then
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
B_{D_j}&=\bigvee_{u\in\mathcal M_j}B_u,\\
B_Q&=\bigwedge_{j=1}^{d}B_{D_j},\\
\mathcal S_Q&=\{pos_i:B_Q[pos_i]=1\}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:p4-conjunctive-result}
\end{equation}
By~\eqref{eq:p4-policy-consistency}, every selected position is both
query-matched and record-authorized. The user submits
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{Req}_Q^{\mathrm{agg}}
=
(e_q,qid,H(B_Q),\mathcal S_Q)
\label{eq:p4-aggregation-request}
\end{equation}
through the authenticated query session.

\paragraph*{Step 3: Query-Bound Encrypted Aggregation}

Before aggregation, the cloud independently reconstructs the
conjunctive bitmap from the authenticated nodes bound to $(e_q,qid)$:
\begin{equation}
B_Q^{*}
=
\bigwedge_{j=1}^{d}
\left(
\bigvee_{u\in\mathcal M_j}B_u
\right),
\qquad
\mathcal S_Q^{*}
=
\{pos_i:B_Q^{*}[pos_i]=1\}.
\label{eq:p4-authorized-selection}
\end{equation}
It proceeds only if
\begin{equation}
H(B_Q)=H(B_Q^{*})
\quad\land\quad
\mathcal S_Q=\mathcal S_Q^{*};
\label{eq:p4-selection-check}
\end{equation}
otherwise, the aggregation request is rejected.

For the validated set $\mathcal S_Q^{*}$, the cloud retrieves the
unique aggregation entries and computes
\begin{equation}
CT_{\mathrm{sum}}
=
\bigoplus_{E,\,pos_i\in\mathcal S_Q^{*}}CT_v^{(i)},
\qquad
CT_{\mathrm{count}}
=
\bigoplus_{E,\,pos_i\in\mathcal S_Q^{*}}CT_1^{(i)}.
\label{eq:p4-encrypted-aggregation}
\end{equation}
It also generates
\begin{equation}
\pi_Q^{\mathrm{agg}}
=
\mathsf{MerkleMultiProof}
\left(
\{\sigma_i^{\mathrm{agg}}\}_{pos_i\in\mathcal S_Q^{*}},
Root_{e_q}^{\mathrm{agg}}
\right).
\label{eq:p4-aggregation-proof}
\end{equation}

\paragraph*{Step 4: Verifiable Response Construction}

Using aggregation entries ordered by $pos_i$, the cloud computes
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\Delta_Q
=
H\bigl(
&\mathtt{RESP}\parallel e_q\parallel qid
\parallel Root_{e_q}\parallel H(B_Q^{*})\\
&\parallel H(\mathsf{enc}(\{\mathcal A_i\}))
\parallel CT_{\mathrm{sum}}
\parallel CT_{\mathrm{count}}
\bigr).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:p4-response-digest}
\end{equation}
After validating $\mathcal S_Q=\mathcal S_Q^{*}$, the gateway
authorizes threshold decryption of this query-bound aggregate by
issuing
\begin{equation}
Cert_Q^{\mathrm{dec}}
=
\mathsf{DS.Sign}_{sk_{\mathrm{DO}}}
\left(
H\bigl(
\mathtt{DEC}\parallel e_q\parallel qid
\parallel\Delta_Q
\parallel CT_{\mathrm{sum}}
\parallel CT_{\mathrm{count}}
\bigr)
\right).
\label{eq:p4-decryption-certificate}
\end{equation}
The cloud returns
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\mathcal{Rsp}_Q^{(2)}
=\bigl(
e_q,qid,H(B_Q^{*}),
CT_{\mathrm{sum}},CT_{\mathrm{count}},\\
\{\mathcal A_i\}_{pos_i\in\mathcal S_Q^{*}},
\pi_Q^{\mathrm{agg}},\Delta_Q,
Cert_Q^{\mathrm{dec}}
\bigr).
\end{split}
\label{eq:p4-final-response}
\end{equation}
Phase~5 verifies the query-bound result, aggregation proof,
recomputed ciphertexts, response digest, and decryption certificate
before requesting threshold decryption.

For optional record retrieval, the cloud returns
\begin{equation}
\left\{
Hdr_i,CT_{\mathrm{AES}}^{(i)},C_i^{\mathrm{rec}}
\right\}_{pos_i\in\mathcal S_Q^{*}},
\label{eq:p4-record-retrieval}
\end{equation}
with record-level authorization enforced by
$\mathsf{ABSE.Dec}$ before AES-GCM decryption.

\subsubsection*{\textbf{Phase 5 --- Query-Bound Verification and Threshold Decryption}}

Upon receiving $\mathcal{Rsp}_Q^{(2)}$, the user verifies that the
response is bound to the authorized query state and validated position
set $\mathcal S_Q^{*}$ established in Phase~4 before requesting
threshold or optional record decryption.

\paragraph*{Step 1: Query-Bound Response Verification}

The user retrieves
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
State_{e_q}
=
\bigl(
&e_q,N_{e_q},\beta,n_{e_q}^{\mathrm{blk}},
Root_{e_q}^{\mathrm{idx}},Root_{e_q}^{\mathrm{agg}},\\
&Root_{e_q},ts_{e_q},\sigma_{\mathrm{DO}}
\bigr)
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:p5-state}
\end{equation}
and verifies
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
Root_{e_q}
&=
H\bigl(
\mathtt{EPOCH}\parallel e_q
\parallel N_{e_q}\parallel\beta
\parallel n_{e_q}^{\mathrm{blk}}\\
&\qquad\quad
\parallel Root_{e_q}^{\mathrm{idx}}
\parallel Root_{e_q}^{\mathrm{agg}}
\bigr),\\
\mathsf{DS.Verify}_{pk_{\mathrm{DO}}}
&\bigl(
H(Root_{e_q}\parallel e_q\parallel ts_{e_q}),
\sigma_{\mathrm{DO}}
\bigr)=1.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:p5-state-verification}
\end{equation}
It confirms that $(e_q,qid)$ is valid, unexpired, and bound to
$T_Q$, and verifies
\begin{equation}
\{pos_i:\mathcal A_i\in\mathcal{Rsp}_Q^{(2)}\}
=
\mathcal S_Q^{*}
=
\{pos_i:B_Q^{*}[pos_i]=1\}.
\label{eq:p5-position-verification}
\end{equation}

For each
$\mathcal A_i=(pos_i,rid_i,e_i,h_i^{\mathrm{rec}},
CT_v^{(i)},CT_1^{(i)})$, the user computes
\begin{equation}
\sigma_i^{\mathrm{agg}}
=
H\bigl(
\mathtt{AGG}\parallel e_i
\parallel\mathsf{enc}(\mathcal A_i)
\bigr)
\label{eq:p5-aggregation-leaf}
\end{equation}
and verifies
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{MerkleMultiVerify}
\left(
\{\sigma_i^{\mathrm{agg}}\}_{pos_i\in\mathcal S_Q^{*}},
\pi_Q^{\mathrm{agg}},
Root_{e_q}^{\mathrm{agg}}
\right)=1.
\label{eq:p5-multiproof-verification}
\end{equation}

The user independently recomputes
\begin{equation}
\widehat{CT}_{\mathrm{sum}}
=
\bigoplus_{E,\,pos_i\in\mathcal S_Q^{*}}CT_v^{(i)},
\qquad
\widehat{CT}_{\mathrm{count}}
=
\bigoplus_{E,\,pos_i\in\mathcal S_Q^{*}}CT_1^{(i)}
\label{eq:p5-aggregate-recomputation}
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\widehat{\Delta}_Q
=
H\bigl(
&\mathtt{RESP}\parallel e_q\parallel qid
\parallel Root_{e_q}\parallel H(B_Q^{*})\\
&\parallel H(\mathsf{enc}(\{\mathcal A_i\}))
\parallel CT_{\mathrm{sum}}
\parallel CT_{\mathrm{count}}
\bigr).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:p5-response-digest}
\end{equation}
The response is accepted only if
\begin{equation}
\widehat{CT}_{\mathrm{sum}}=CT_{\mathrm{sum}}
\land
\widehat{CT}_{\mathrm{count}}=CT_{\mathrm{count}}
\land
\widehat{\Delta}_Q=\Delta_Q.
\label{eq:p5-response-acceptance}
\end{equation}

\paragraph*{Step 2: Query-Authorized and Verifiable Threshold Decryption}

After validating $\mathcal S_Q=\mathcal S_Q^{*}$ and constructing the
aggregate response in Phase~4, the gateway issues
\begin{equation}
Cert_Q^{\mathrm{dec}}
=
\mathsf{DS.Sign}_{sk_{\mathrm{DO}}}
\left(
H\bigl(
\mathtt{DEC}\parallel e_q\parallel qid
\parallel\Delta_Q
\parallel CT_{\mathrm{sum}}
\parallel CT_{\mathrm{count}}
\bigr)
\right).
\label{eq:p5-decryption-certificate}
\end{equation}
This certificate authorizes threshold decryption only for the
aggregate ciphertexts bound to the validated query response.

The user submits
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{Req}_Q^{\mathrm{dec}}
=
(e_q,qid,\Delta_Q,CT_{\mathrm{sum}},
CT_{\mathrm{count}},Cert_Q^{\mathrm{dec}})
\label{eq:p5-decryption-request}
\end{equation}
to the TDAs. Before generating a partial decryption, each TDA verifies
that $(e_q,qid)$ is valid and unexpired and that
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{DS.Verify}_{pk_{\mathrm{DO}}}
\left(
H\bigl(
\mathtt{DEC}\parallel e_q\parallel qid
\parallel\Delta_Q
\parallel CT_{\mathrm{sum}}
\parallel CT_{\mathrm{count}}
\bigr),
Cert_Q^{\mathrm{dec}}
\right)=1.
\label{eq:p5-decryption-authorization}
\end{equation}
Thus, a TDA rejects any aggregate ciphertext not bound to the
policy-sanctioned query response established in Phase~4.

For $CT_z=(C_1^z,C_2^z)$,
$z\in\{\mathrm{sum},\mathrm{count}\}$, each TDA
$\ell\in S$, $|S|\geq t$, returns
\begin{equation}
\delta_\ell^z
=
sk_\ell C_1^z,
\qquad
\pi_\ell^z
=
\mathsf{DLEQ.Prove}
(sk_\ell;G,pk_\ell,C_1^z,\delta_\ell^z).
\label{eq:p5-partial-decryption}
\end{equation}
The user accepts a share only if
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{DLEQ.Verify}
(G,pk_\ell,C_1^z,\delta_\ell^z,\pi_\ell^z)=1
\label{eq:p5-dleq-verification}
\end{equation}
and combines at least $t$ valid shares:
\begin{equation}
D_z
=
\sum_{\ell\in S}\lambda_\ell\delta_\ell^z,
\qquad
\lambda_\ell
=
\prod_{\substack{k\in S\\k\neq\ell}}
\frac{k}{k-\ell}
\bmod q_E.
\label{eq:p5-share-combination}
\end{equation}
The aggregate plaintexts are recovered as
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
M_z&=C_2^z-D_z,\\
Sum&=\mathsf{BSGS}(M_{\mathrm{sum}},\mathcal V_{\mathrm{sum}}),\\
Count&=\mathsf{BSGS}(M_{\mathrm{count}},[0,N_{e_q}]),
\end{aligned}
\qquad
Count=|\mathcal S_Q^{*}|.
\label{eq:p5-aggregate-recovery}
\end{equation}
For $Count>0$,
\begin{equation}
Avg=\frac{Sum}{Count}.
\label{eq:p5-average}
\end{equation}
If $Count=0$, the result is empty and $Avg$ is undefined.

\paragraph*{Step 3: Optional Record Decryption}

For each requested $pos_i\in\mathcal S_Q^{*}$ and
$Hdr_i=(ID_i,rid_i,seq_i,N_i,e_i)$, the user verifies
\begin{equation}
H\bigl(
\mathsf{enc}(Hdr_i)\parallel
CT_{\mathrm{AES}}^{(i)}
\parallel C_i^{\mathrm{rec}}
\bigr)
=
h_i^{\mathrm{rec}}
\label{eq:p5-record-verification}
\end{equation}
and computes
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
K_i^{\mathrm{rec}}
&=
\mathsf{ABSE.Dec}
(SK_{\mathcal A},C_i^{\mathrm{rec}},e_i),\\
D_i
&=
\mathsf{AES\mbox{-}GCM.Dec}_{K_i^{\mathrm{rec}}}
(N_i,CT_{\mathrm{AES}}^{(i)};\mathsf{enc}(Hdr_i)).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:p5-record-decryption}
\end{equation}
ABSE decryption succeeds only when
$\mathcal A\models\mathcal P_i$. The result is accepted only if all
query-binding, blockchain, Merkle, aggregation, DLEQ,
bounded-recovery, and AES-GCM checks succeed.
\section{Security Analysis}

\subsection{Security Model}

We consider a probabilistic polynomial-time (PPT) adversary
$\mathcal A$ that controls the cloud and communication channels. The
cloud may inspect its permitted leakage and arbitrarily omit, modify,
substitute, fabricate, or replay protocol messages. The Edge Gateway
is honest-but-curious and non-colluding: it follows the protocol but
observes sensor identities, canonical-node metadata, bitmap
memberships, and update patterns. Completeness is therefore defined
relative to the state committed by the gateway.

Unauthorized users may collude with the cloud and other users, while
fewer than $t$ TDAs may be corrupted. The adversary does not obtain
$MSK$, $K_{\mathrm{sel}}$, $sk_{\mathrm{DO}}$, an attribute key
satisfying an unauthorized policy, or at least $t$ valid threshold
shares. The blockchain is assumed to provide immutable finalized
states, cryptographic nonces are never reused, and protocol messages
are exchanged through authenticated channels.

BVCRSA composes a policy-controlled searchable-encryption component
with an attribute-based KEM, as specified in Phase~1. The searchable
component is assumed to provide adaptive keyword privacy, randomized
context-bound tokens, policy enforcement, and user-collusion
resistance, while the attribute-based KEM provides IND-CPA
payload-key confidentiality and collusion-resistant policy
enforcement. Query tokens are bound to $(e_c,qid)$, whereas
encapsulated node and record keys are bound to their corresponding
epoch $e_c$. The security claims below are conditional on these
underlying component assumptions.


The security objectives of BVCRSA are
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
&\mathsf{PacketAuthenticity}
\wedge\mathsf{QueryPrivacy}
\wedge\mathsf{DataConfidentiality}\\
&\wedge\mathsf{PolicyEnforcement}
\wedge\mathsf{ThresholdSecurity}\\
&\wedge\mathsf{AggregationCorrectness}
\wedge\mathsf{StateIntegrity}
\wedge\mathsf{Freshness}.
\end{split}
\label{eq:security-objectives}
\end{equation}
BVCRSA does not protect against denial of service, a malicious
gateway that commits incorrect data, collusion between the gateway
and cloud, compromise of at least $t$ TDAs, or leakage explicitly
permitted below.

\subsection{Leakage Profile}

BVCRSA provides data and query-content confidentiality subject to the
structural leakage required for encrypted indexing, search, and
aggregation. Its cloud-side leakage is
\begin{equation}
\mathcal L_{\mathrm{BVCRSA}}
=
\left(
\mathcal L_{\mathrm{Setup}},
\mathcal L_{\mathrm{Data}},
\mathcal L_{\mathrm{Query}},
\mathcal L_{\mathrm{Access}}
\right).
\label{eq:leakage-profile}
\end{equation}

Public parameters and finalized epoch states reveal
\begin{equation}
\mathcal L_{\mathrm{Setup}}
=
\left(
\mathsf{Pub},
\{e_q,N_{e_q},\beta,n_{e_q}^{\mathrm{blk}},
Root_{e_q},ts_{e_q},\sigma_{\mathrm{DO}}\}
\right).
\label{eq:setup-leakage}
\end{equation}

During outsourcing, the cloud observes record headers, index topology,
ciphertext and bitmap sizes, aggregation structure, policy shapes,
and update patterns:
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathcal L_{\mathrm{Data}}
=
\bigl(
&N_{e_q},N_u,\beta,n_{e_q}^{\mathrm{blk}},
\{Hdr_i,pos_i,h_i^{\mathrm{rec}}\},\{D_j,u\},\\
&\{|CT_{\mathrm{AES}}^{(i)}|,|C_i^{\mathrm{rec}}|,
|CT_v^{(i)}|,|CT_1^{(i)}|\},
\{|I_u|,|C_u^K|\},\\
&\{|\widetilde B_{u,b}|,\nu_{u,b}\},
\{|\mathcal P_u|,|\mathcal P_i|\},
\mathsf{UpdPat}
\bigr),
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:data-leakage}
\end{equation}
where $Hdr_i=(ID_i,rid_i,seq_i,N_i,e_i)$. If the instantiated ABSE
does not hide access structures, the policies themselves are included
in $\mathcal L_{\mathrm{Data}}$.

For each query, the cloud observes
\begin{equation}
\mathcal L_{\mathrm{Query}}
=
\left(
d,\{\ell_j\}_{j=1}^{d},|T_Q|,
\mathsf{QP}_Q,\mathcal U_Q
\right),
\label{eq:query-leakage}
\end{equation}
where $\mathsf{QP}_Q$ captures token equality, repetition, and
frequency patterns, while $\mathcal U_Q$ identifies matched canonical
nodes. Since these nodes represent fixed numerical intervals,
$\mathcal U_Q$ may reveal partial or complete range-query semantics.

The second round further reveals
\begin{equation}
\mathcal L_{\mathrm{Access}}
=
\left(
\mathcal S_Q^{*},|\mathcal S_Q^{*}|,
H(B_Q^{*}),\mathsf{AP}_Q,
\mathsf{Ret}_Q,\mathsf{RespSize}_Q
\right),
\label{eq:access-leakage}
\end{equation}
where $\mathsf{AP}_Q$ captures repeated or overlapping position
patterns and $\mathsf{Ret}_Q\subseteq\mathcal S_Q^{*}$ denotes
retrieved positions. Thus, the cloud learns result cardinality and
may correlate queries through node, position, frequency, retrieval,
and response-size patterns, but does not directly recover
$\{B_u\}$ or $B_Q^{*}$ beyond this leakage.

The honest-but-curious gateway additionally observes
\begin{equation}
\mathcal L_{\mathrm{GW}}
=
\left(
\{Hdr_i,Meta_i,pos_i\},
N_{e_q},\beta,n_{e_q}^{\mathrm{blk}},
\mathsf{UpdPat},\text{bitmap memberships}
\right).
\label{eq:gateway-leakage}
\end{equation}
Hence, gateway confidentiality protects complete records,
record-specific keys, and aggregate plaintexts, but not metadata and
bitmap memberships required for index construction.

An authorized user satisfying $\mathcal P_u$ additionally learns
\begin{equation}
\mathcal L_{\mathrm{User}}
=
\left(
\{u,B_u\}_{u\in\mathcal U_Q},
B_Q^{*},\mathcal S_Q^{*}
\right).
\label{eq:user-leakage}
\end{equation}
Since a canonical node may cover a wider interval than the final
conjunctive result, $B_u$ reveals membership within that interval and
may permit approximate value-range inference outside
$\mathcal S_Q^{*}$. Record contents and keys remain independently
protected by $\mathcal P_i$.

BVCRSA therefore does not claim ORAM/PIR-style access-pattern
obliviousness or membership privacy against users authorized for the
corresponding nodes. It protects query contents, cryptographic tokens,
record contents, and keys beyond the declared leakage. Hiding matched
nodes, access patterns, or non-result memberships would require
additional oblivious or query-scoped disclosure mechanisms.

\subsection{Theorem 1: Sensor-Packet Authenticity}

\textbf{Theorem 1.}
Assuming the EUF-CMA security of HMAC and monotonically increasing
sequence numbers, a PPT adversary cannot forge, modify, or replay an
accepted sensor packet except with negligible probability.

\textbf{Proof.}
Sensor $i$ authenticates
\begin{equation}
Tag_i
=
\mathsf{HMAC}_{K_{\mathrm{HMAC}}^{(i)}}
\left(
\mathsf{enc}(\mathcal P_i\setminus\{Tag_i\})
\right).
\label{eq:sec-sensor-tag}
\end{equation}
The gateway accepts $\mathcal P_i$ only if
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
&\mathsf{HMAC.Verify}_{K_{\mathrm{HMAC}}^{(i)}}
\left(
\mathsf{enc}(\mathcal P_i\setminus\{Tag_i\}),Tag_i
\right)=1\\
&\qquad\land\; seq_i>seq_i^{\mathrm{last}} .
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:sec-packet-acceptance}
\end{equation}
Any modification to $rid_i$, $N_i$, $CT_{\mathrm{AES}}^{(i)}$,
$C_i^{\mathrm{rec}}$, $CT_v^{(i)}$, or $Meta_i$ requires a new valid
tag and therefore constitutes an HMAC forgery. Replaying a previously
accepted packet fails the sequence-number check. Hence,
\begin{equation}
\Pr[\mathsf{PacketForge}]
\leq
\mathsf{Adv}_{\mathcal A}^{\mathrm{HMAC\mbox{-}EUF\mbox{-}CMA}}
(\lambda)
+
\operatorname{negl}(\lambda).
\label{eq:packet-authenticity-bound}
\end{equation}
\hfill$\square$

\subsection{Theorem 2: Conjunctive Query Privacy}

\textbf{Theorem 2.}
Assuming adaptive keyword privacy of the instantiated
policy-controlled searchable-encryption component, for any PPT cloud
adversary $\mathcal A$, there exists a PPT simulator $\mathcal S$
whose view is computationally indistinguishable from the real BVCRSA
query execution given only $\mathcal L_{\mathrm{Query}}$ and
$\mathcal L_{\mathrm{Access}}$. Thus, BVCRSA hides query contents
beyond the explicitly defined leakage.

\textbf{Proof.}
Let $\mathsf{Real}_{\mathcal A}$ denote the cloud's view in a real
execution and $\mathsf{Ideal}_{\mathcal A,\mathcal S}$ the view
generated by $\mathcal S$. Given
\begin{equation}
\mathcal L_{\mathrm{Query}}
=
\left(
d,\{\ell_j\}_{j=1}^{d},|T_Q|,
\mathsf{QP}_Q,\mathcal U_Q
\right),
\label{eq:sec-query-leakage}
\end{equation}
$\mathcal S$ generates syntactically valid simulated indexes and
tokens consistent with the disclosed token-group sizes, equality and
repetition pattern $\mathsf{QP}_Q$, and matched-node set
$\mathcal U_Q$. Adaptive keyword privacy implies that replacing the
real searchable objects with these simulated values is
computationally indistinguishable.

Given
\begin{equation}
\mathcal L_{\mathrm{Access}}
=
\left(
\mathcal S_Q^{*},|\mathcal S_Q^{*}|,H(B_Q^{*}),
\mathsf{AP}_Q,\mathsf{Ret}_Q,\mathsf{RespSize}_Q
\right),
\label{eq:sec-access-leakage}
\end{equation}
$\mathcal S$ generates the remaining response view consistently with
the disclosed selected positions, access and retrieval patterns, and
response sizes. Hence,
\begin{equation}
\left|
\Pr[\mathsf{Real}_{\mathcal A}=1]
-
\Pr[\mathsf{Ideal}_{\mathcal A,\mathcal S}=1]
\right|
\leq
\mathsf{Adv}_{\mathcal A}^{\mathrm{SE}}(\lambda)
+
\operatorname{negl}(\lambda).
\label{eq:query-privacy-bound}
\end{equation}

Importantly, $\mathcal U_Q$ is explicit leakage. Since canonical nodes
represent fixed numerical intervals, observing $\mathcal U_Q$ may
reveal partial or, in some cases, complete range-query semantics.
Likewise, $\mathsf{QP}_Q$ and $\mathsf{AP}_Q$ permit query and access
linkability. Therefore, the theorem establishes query-content privacy
only beyond the declared leakage and does not claim range- or
access-pattern obliviousness.
\hfill$\square$
\subsection{Theorem 3: Outsourced-Data Confidentiality}

\textbf{Theorem 3.}
Assuming the pseudorandomness of $\mathsf{KDF}$, the IND-CPA
security of the attribute-based KEM, the AEAD security of AES-GCM,
the pseudorandomness of the bitmap PRF/PRG, and the IND-CPA security
of Threshold EC-ElGamal, the cloud learns no sensor record, bitmap
content, or aggregation value beyond
$\mathcal L_{\mathrm{BVCRSA}}$.

\textbf{Proof.}
We use a sequence of games.

\textbf{Game $G_0$.}
This is the real protocol execution.

\textbf{Game $G_1$.}
Replace each derived record key
\begin{equation}
K_i^{\mathrm{rec}}
=
\mathsf{KDF}
(K_{\mathrm{AES}}^{(i)},rid_i\parallel N_i)
\label{eq:sec-record-key}
\end{equation}
with an independent uniform key. Distinguishing $G_1$ from $G_0$
breaks the pseudorandomness of $\mathsf{KDF}$.

\textbf{Game $G_2$.}
For policies not satisfied by the adversary, replace the
attribute-based KEM envelopes $C_i^{\mathrm{rec}}$ and $C_u^K$
with encapsulations of fixed equal-length keys. Distinguishing
$G_2$ from $G_1$ breaks the IND-CPA security of the
attribute-based KEM.

\textbf{Game $G_3$.}
Replace each AES-GCM ciphertext with an encryption of an equal-length
zero string. Since $N_i$ is unique and the associated data are
authenticated, distinguishing $G_3$ from $G_2$ breaks the AEAD
security of AES-GCM.

\textbf{Game $G_4$.}
Replace every protected bitmap mask
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{PRG}
\left(
F(K_u,b\parallel\nu_{u,b}),|B_{u,b}|
\right)
\label{eq:sec-bitmap-mask}
\end{equation}
with a uniform string of equal length. Distinguishing this replacement
breaks the pseudorandomness of $F$ or $\mathsf{PRG}$.

\textbf{Game $G_5$.}
Replace each Threshold EC-ElGamal ciphertext
\begin{equation}
CT_v^{(i)}
=
(r_iG,v_iG+r_i pk_{\mathrm{AHE}})
\label{eq:sec-value-ciphertext}
\end{equation}
with an encryption of a fixed value. Distinguishing this game breaks
the IND-CPA security of Threshold EC-ElGamal when fewer than $t$
shares are corrupted.

The view in $G_5$ depends only on the declared leakage. Hence,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathsf{Adv}*{\mathcal A}^{\mathrm{Conf}}
\leq{}&
\mathsf{Adv}^{\mathrm{KDF}}
+\mathsf{Adv}^{\mathrm{ABKEM\mbox{-}IND\mbox{-}CPA}}
+\mathsf{Adv}^{\mathrm{AEAD}}\
&+
\mathsf{Adv}^{\mathrm{PRF/PRG}}
+\mathsf{Adv}^{\mathrm{T\mbox{-}ECElGamal}}
+\operatorname{negl}(\lambda).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:data-confidentiality-bound}
\end{equation}
This result applies to the cloud under the non-collusion assumption.
The gateway separately learns the metadata specified by
$\mathcal L*{\mathrm{GW}}$.
\hfill$\square$


\subsection{Theorem 4: Policy Enforcement and Anti-Collusion}

\textbf{Theorem 4.}
Assume the instantiated ABSE satisfies search soundness,
encapsulated-key confidentiality, and collusion resistance, and that
index construction enforces
$pos_i\in B_u\Rightarrow\mathcal P_u\Rightarrow\mathcal P_i$.
Then BVCRSA permits node search, aggregation, and record recovery only
under the corresponding authorized policies. Moreover, colluding users
cannot combine independently issued credentials to satisfy a node- or
record-level policy that none satisfies individually, except with
negligible probability.

\textbf{Proof.}
For node $u$, an accepted search requires
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{ABSE.Test}
(PP,I_u,Tok_{j,i},e_q,qid)=1,
\qquad
\mathcal A\models\mathcal P_u,
\label{eq:sec-node-authorization}
\end{equation}
by ABSE search soundness and policy enforcement. Recovery of the
corresponding node key additionally requires
\begin{equation}
K_u=
\mathsf{ABSE.Dec}(SK_{\mathcal A},C_u^K,e_q).
\label{eq:sec-node-key-recovery}
\end{equation}

For every record represented by node $u$, the index-construction
invariant gives
\begin{equation}
pos_i\in B_u
\land
\mathcal P_u\Rightarrow\mathcal P_i.
\label{eq:sec-policy-consistency}
\end{equation}
Therefore,
\begin{equation}
\mathcal A\models\mathcal P_u
\land pos_i\in B_u
\Longrightarrow
\mathcal A\models\mathcal P_i,
\label{eq:sec-aggregation-authorization}
\end{equation}
so an authorized node cannot introduce into the aggregation set a
record protected by a stronger incompatible policy.

Record recovery is independently protected by
\begin{equation}
C_i^{\mathrm{rec}}
=
\mathsf{ABSE.Enc}
(PP,K_i^{\mathrm{rec}},\mathcal P_i,e_i),
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
K_i^{\mathrm{rec}}
=
\mathsf{ABSE.Dec}
(SK_{\mathcal A},C_i^{\mathrm{rec}},e_i)
\label{eq:sec-record-authorization}
\end{equation}
succeeds only for $\mathcal A\models\mathcal P_i$ at the bound epoch.

Finally, by the collusion resistance of the instantiated ABSE,
independently randomized keys
$SK_{\mathcal A_1},\ldots,SK_{\mathcal A_c}$ cannot be combined to
produce a valid authorized match or recover a protected key for a
policy that no colluding user satisfies individually. Consequently,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathsf{Adv}_{\mathcal A}^{\mathrm{Pol}}
\leq{}&
\mathsf{Adv}_{\mathcal A}^{\mathrm{ABSE\mbox{-}Sound}}
+
\mathsf{Adv}_{\mathcal A}^{\mathrm{ABSE\mbox{-}KConf}}\\
&+
\mathsf{Adv}_{\mathcal A}^{\mathrm{ABSE\mbox{-}Coll}}
+\operatorname{negl}(\lambda).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:policy-security-bound}
\end{equation}
Hence, under the explicitly stated security properties of the
instantiated ABSE, BVCRSA enforces consistent node- and record-level
authorization for search, aggregation, and record recovery, including
against colluding users.
\hfill$\square$
\subsection{Theorem 5: Query-Authorized Threshold-Decryption Security}

\textbf{Theorem 5.}
Assuming EUF-CMA security of the gateway signature, threshold security
of EC-ElGamal, and soundness of the DLEQ proof, TDAs issue partial
decryptions only for aggregate ciphertexts bound to a valid,
unexpired, query-authorized response. Moreover, fewer than $t$ TDAs
cannot decrypt an aggregate ciphertext, and an invalid partial
decryption is detected except with negligible probability.

\textbf{Proof.}
For an accepted aggregate, the gateway issues
\begin{equation}
Cert_Q^{\mathrm{dec}}
=
\mathsf{DS.Sign}_{sk_{\mathrm{DO}}}
\left(
H\bigl(
\mathtt{DEC}\parallel e_q\parallel qid
\parallel\Delta_Q
\parallel CT_{\mathrm{sum}}
\parallel CT_{\mathrm{count}}
\bigr)
\right)
\label{eq:sec-decryption-certificate}
\end{equation}
only after validating the query-bound selection
$\mathcal S_Q=\mathcal S_Q^{*}$. Before producing a share, each TDA
verifies that $(e_q,qid)$ is valid and unexpired and that
$Cert_Q^{\mathrm{dec}}$ authenticates the submitted aggregate
ciphertexts. Hence, obtaining partial decryptions for an aggregate
not bound to the authorized query response requires forging the
gateway signature, except with negligible probability.

For $CT_z=(C_1^z,C_2^z)$,
$z\in\{\mathrm{sum},\mathrm{count}\}$, TDA $\ell$ computes
\begin{equation}
\delta_\ell^z=sk_\ell C_1^z
\label{eq:sec-partial-decryption}
\end{equation}
and proves
\begin{equation}
\log_G(pk_\ell)
=
\log_{C_1^z}(\delta_\ell^z)
\label{eq:sec-dleq-relation}
\end{equation}
using a Chaum--Pedersen DLEQ proof. By threshold EC-ElGamal security,
fewer than $t$ TDAs cannot reconstruct the decryption term
\begin{equation}
D_z
=
\sum_{\ell\in S}\lambda_\ell\delta_\ell^z
=
xC_1^z,
\qquad |S|\geq t,
\label{eq:sec-share-reconstruction}
\end{equation}
and therefore cannot recover $M_z=C_2^z-D_z$. DLEQ soundness further
prevents an invalid partial decryption from passing verification.

Consequently,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\mathsf{Adv}_{\mathcal A}^{\mathrm{TDec}}
\leq{}&
\mathsf{Adv}_{\mathcal A}^{\mathrm{EUF\mbox{-}CMA}}
+
\mathsf{Adv}_{\mathcal A}^{\mathrm{T\mbox{-}ECElGamal}}\\
&+
\mathsf{Adv}_{\mathcal A}^{\mathrm{DLEQ}}
+
\operatorname{negl}(\lambda).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:threshold-security-bound}
\end{equation}
Thus, threshold decryption is restricted to query-authorized
aggregates, while threshold secrecy and verifiable partial
decryption are preserved.
\hfill$\square$

\subsection{Theorem 6: Query- and Policy-Bound Verifiable Aggregation}

\textbf{Theorem 6.}
Assume the instantiated ABSE satisfies search soundness and policy
enforcement, and the Merkle commitments are sound. Relative to the
gateway-committed state, BVCRSA accepts aggregation only over the
authenticated conjunctive-query result bound to $(e_q,qid)$ and
authorized under the corresponding record policies. Every accepted
aggregate contains exactly the authenticated ciphertexts of this set,
with each selected position contributing once.

\textbf{Proof.}
For each query token, the corresponding canonical node must satisfy
the instantiated ABSE test and its index Merkle proof. Completeness
and validity of the committed bitmap blocks then uniquely determine
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
B_{D_j}
&=\bigvee_{u\in\mathcal M_j}B_u,
\qquad
B_Q^{*}=\bigwedge_{j=1}^{d}B_{D_j},\\
\mathcal S_Q^{*}
&=\{pos_i:B_Q^{*}[pos_i]=1\}.
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:sec-conjunctive-result}
\end{equation}
By ABSE search soundness and the policy-consistency invariant of
Theorem~4, every $pos_i\in\mathcal S_Q^{*}$ is both query-matched and
authorized under its record policy $\mathcal P_i$.

The second-round request is accepted only if
\begin{equation}
(e_q,qid)\text{ is valid}
\quad\land\quad
\mathcal S_Q=\mathcal S_Q^{*},
\label{eq:sec-query-bound-selection}
\end{equation}
thereby rejecting arbitrary or unauthorized position sets.

For each selected aggregation entry $\mathcal A_i$, the user verifies
its Merkle multi-proof against $Root_{e_q}^{\mathrm{agg}}$ and
requires
\begin{equation}
\{pos_i:\mathcal A_i\in\mathcal{Rsp}_Q^{(2)}\}
=
\mathcal S_Q^{*}.
\label{eq:sec-aggregation-position-set}
\end{equation}
The user independently recomputes
\begin{equation}
CT_{\mathrm{sum}}
=
\bigoplus_{E,\,pos_i\in\mathcal S_Q^{*}}CT_v^{(i)},
\qquad
CT_{\mathrm{count}}
=
\bigoplus_{E,\,pos_i\in\mathcal S_Q^{*}}CT_1^{(i)},
\label{eq:sec-aggregation-correctness}
\end{equation}
and verifies their binding to $\Delta_Q$. The gateway issues
$Cert_Q^{\mathrm{dec}}$ only for this validated query-bound
aggregate; hence, by Theorem~5, the TDAs reject aggregate
ciphertexts not authorized for $(e_q,qid)$. After threshold
decryption, the user additionally verifies
\begin{equation}
Count=|\mathcal S_Q^{*}|.
\label{eq:sec-count-correctness}
\end{equation}

Therefore, insertion, omission, substitution, duplication, or
unauthorized selection of aggregate contributions is detected except
with negligible probability. Every accepted aggregate consequently
contains exactly the authenticated, query-matched, and
policy-authorized ciphertexts, each counted once, relative to the
gateway-committed state.
\hfill$\square$

\subsection{Theorem 7: Query-State Integrity, Freshness, and Rollback Resistance}

\textbf{Theorem 7.}
Assuming EUF-CMA security of the gateway signature, collision
resistance of $H$, Merkle-proof soundness, search soundness of the
instantiated ABSE, and blockchain finality, a malicious cloud cannot
modify, omit, substitute, replay, or roll back an accepted BVCRSA
query response without detection, except with negligible probability.

\textbf{Proof.}
For each finalized epoch $e_q$, the gateway commits to
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
Root_{e_q}
=
H\bigl(
&\mathtt{EPOCH}\parallel e_q
\parallel N_{e_q}\parallel\beta
\parallel n_{e_q}^{\mathrm{blk}}\\
&\parallel Root_{e_q}^{\mathrm{idx}}
\parallel Root_{e_q}^{\mathrm{agg}}
\bigr)
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:sec-epoch-state}
\end{equation}
and authenticates it by
\begin{equation}
\sigma_{\mathrm{DO}}
=
\mathsf{DS.Sign}_{sk_{\mathrm{DO}}}
\bigl(H(Root_{e_q}\parallel e_q\parallel ts_{e_q})\bigr).
\label{eq:sec-epoch-signature}
\end{equation}
Thus, altering the committed state requires a hash collision,
signature forgery, or violation of blockchain finality.

By ABSE search soundness and Merkle-proof soundness, each returned
canonical node must be a valid query match committed under
$Root_{e_q}^{\mathrm{idx}}$. For every matched node, the user further
requires
\begin{equation}
\{b:\widetilde B_{u,b}\text{ is returned}\}
=
\{1,\ldots,n_{e_q}^{\mathrm{blk}}\},
\label{eq:sec-block-set-integrity}
\end{equation}
with all blocks authenticated against $Root_u^{\mathrm{bmp}}$.
Hence, omission, duplication, substitution, truncation, or
modification of authenticated search data is detected.

By Theorem~6, aggregation is restricted to the authenticated,
query- and policy-bound set $\mathcal S_Q^{*}$. The digest
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\Delta_Q
=
H\bigl(
&\mathtt{RESP}\parallel e_q\parallel qid
\parallel Root_{e_q}\parallel H(B_Q^{*})\\
&\parallel H(\mathsf{enc}(\{\mathcal A_i\}))
\parallel CT_{\mathrm{sum}}
\parallel CT_{\mathrm{count}}
\bigr)
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:sec-response-digest}
\end{equation}
binds the verified result and aggregate ciphertexts to the committed
query state. Any inconsistent response therefore fails the Merkle,
position-set, digest, or aggregate-recomputation checks.

Finally,
\begin{equation}
qid
=
H(e_q\parallel Root_{e_q}\parallel
t_{\mathrm{exp}}\parallel n_q)
\label{eq:sec-query-binding}
\end{equation}
binds the execution to a unique nonce, finalized epoch, and validity
period. Cross-query or cross-epoch substitution violates this binding;
expired responses and reused completed $qid$ values are rejected.
Thus, the finalized state $e_q$ remains pinned for the valid query
lifetime, preventing replay and rollback to another epoch.

Consequently,
\begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
\Pr[\mathsf{Forge}]
\leq{}&
\mathsf{Adv}_{\mathsf{DS}}^{\mathrm{EUF\mbox{-}CMA}}
+\mathsf{Adv}_{H}^{\mathrm{CR}}
+\mathsf{Adv}^{\mathrm{Merkle}}\\
&+\mathsf{Adv}^{\mathrm{ABSE\mbox{-}Sound}}
+\mathsf{Adv}_{\mathrm{BC}}^{\mathrm{Finality}}
+\operatorname{negl}(\lambda).
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:query-state-security-bound}
\end{equation}
Hence, BVCRSA provides query-state integrity, freshness, and rollback
resistance relative to the gateway-committed state.
\hfill$\square$


\subsection{Security Scope and Limitations}

BVCRSA authenticates query results relative to the state constructed
by the honest-but-curious, non-colluding Edge Gateway. Because the
gateway exclusively holds $K_{\mathrm{sel}}$, it is trusted for
correct node-key derivation, index construction, and bitmap formation.
Blockchain anchoring authenticates this committed state but cannot
detect records omitted, misclassified, or incorrectly indexed before
commitment. A malicious or compromised gateway is therefore outside
the present threat model.

BVCRSA also leaks canonical-node matches, query repetition, selected
positions, and result cardinality. Eliminating such leakage would
require mechanisms such as ORAM, PIR, zero-knowledge proofs, or
trusted hardware.

User and forward revocation are outside the present scope. Since
\begin{equation}
K_u=F(K_{\mathrm{sel}},D_j\parallel u)
\label{eq:static-node-key-limitation}
\end{equation}
is stable across epochs, a previously authorized user retaining
$K_u$ may decrypt subsequently obtained blocks of the same node.
Forward revocation would require epoch-evolving node keys with
re-encapsulation or revocation-aware key updates.

Finally, canonical-node bitmaps introduce a storage--communication
trade-off. Authenticated fixed-size blocks enable incremental updates
and verification, but bitmap storage grows with the number of nodes
and indexed records, while broad queries may increase communication
and client-memory costs. Compressed, sparse, or query-scoped
authenticated bitmaps are left for future work.
