\subsection{Storage Overhead Analysis}

BVCRSA stores encrypted sensor records, authenticated
range-cover indexes, protected bitmaps, aggregation
ciphertexts, and blockchain commitments. Let $N$ denote the
number of outsourced records and $m_c$ the total number of
canonical interval nodes. The encrypted sensor records incur
$O(N)$ storage, while the authenticated range-cover index
requires $O(m_c)$ searchable-node ciphertexts and Merkle-tree
leaf commitments. Protected bitmaps require
$O(m_cN)$ bits because each canonical node maintains a
fixed-length bitmap over the indexed record space.
Aggregation metadata require one EC-ElGamal ciphertext per
record, yielding $O(N)$ additional storage. Finally, the
blockchain stores only compact epoch-level commitments
consisting of authenticated Merkle roots and signatures,
resulting in $O(E)$ on-chain storage for $E$ finalized
epochs. Consequently, the storage overhead is dominated by
the bitmap index, while blockchain storage remains
independent of the outsourced dataset size.

\subsection{Performance Evaluation}

We evaluate BVCRSA in terms of index construction, trapdoor
generation and conjunctive range search, encrypted aggregation,
and blockchain-assisted verification over outsourced IIoT data.

BVCRSA is compared with representative searchable-encryption and
range-query schemes, including Scheme~\cite{ref25},
Trinity~\cite{ref26}, ABSE-Range~\cite{ref27},
Latt-IBEKS~\cite{ref28}, and VC-KASE~\cite{ref16}.
Because these schemes provide different functionality, comparisons
are restricted to equivalent supported operations under common
workloads and comparable security settings. Unless explicitly
stated, reported query-processing latency excludes network
communication, blockchain access, homomorphic aggregation,
client-side verification, and threshold decryption, which are
measured separately where applicable. BVCRSA's encrypted aggregation
and threshold-decryption functionality, unsupported by the baselines,
is therefore evaluated independently. Accordingly, the comparative
results should be interpreted as operation-level rather than
end-to-end system comparisons, reflecting the trade-off between
BVCRSA's additional security functionality and its associated
verification, communication, and storage overhead.
\subsubsection*{\textbf{Experimental Setup}}

A BVCRSA prototype was implemented in Python~3.12 using
\texttt{py\_ecc} over BN128 for attribute-based searchable
encryption, NIST P-256 for Threshold EC-ElGamal,
\texttt{PyCryptodome} for AES-GCM, and SHA-256/HMAC.
Blockchain-assisted verification used Solidity and
\texttt{Web3.py} on a private Ethereum Clique-PoA network with
a 2-s block interval and one-block finality.

The testbed comprised a Raspberry Pi~4 Model~B (4~GB RAM) for
sensor operations, an Intel Core~i7 laptop (16~GB RAM) for
trapdoor generation, and an AWS cloud instance with 32~GB RAM
and Ubuntu~22.04~LTS for indexing, query processing, aggregation,
and verification. Synthetic IIoT datasets contained 20 sensor
categories with values uniformly distributed over $[0,100]$.
The primary experiments used $N=10^3$--$2\times10^4$ records,
10\%--80\% query selectivity, and $d=1$--5 queried dimensions;
only the query-scalability experiment was extended to $N=10^5$.

Query-processing latency includes canonical-node decomposition,
ABSE $\mathsf{Test}$ operations, authenticated bitmap reconstruction,
and conjunctive evaluation; it is not an end-to-end latency measure.
Network transmission, aggregation, client-side verification,
blockchain interaction, threshold decryption, and bounded BSGS
recovery are excluded from this metric and evaluated separately where
applicable. Blockchain overhead is measured through transaction gas,
payload size, and confirmation latency. Sensor-side experiments
measure cryptographic latency, peak memory, and serialized output
size on the Raspberry Pi.

Each setting was repeated 20 times; unless otherwise stated, results
report the mean with $\pm1$ standard-deviation error bars. Network
delay and sensor energy consumption are excluded. Energy is not
estimated because calibrated power instrumentation was unavailable.

\paragraph*{Baseline Configuration.}
Trinity~\cite{ref26}, ABSE-Range~\cite{ref27},
Latt-IBEKS~\cite{ref28}, and VC-KASE~\cite{ref16} were independently
reimplemented following their published algorithms and parameters,
with adaptations only for the common testbed. All schemes used the
same datasets, workloads, hardware, and software, with approximately
comparable security levels where supported. Comparisons include only
functionality provided by each baseline; unsupported capabilities
were not emulated. Equivalent protocol stages were measured under
identical conditions, excluding scheme-specific preprocessing,
communication, and blockchain costs unless stated otherwise.

Query-processing latency includes canonical-node decomposition,
ABSE $\mathsf{Test}$, authenticated bitmap reconstruction, and
conjunctive evaluation. Aggregation, client-side and blockchain
verification, threshold decryption, and bounded BSGS recovery are
measured separately. Sensor-side measurements cover ABSE key
encapsulation, Threshold EC-ElGamal encryption, AES-GCM, and HMAC,
with latency, peak memory, and serialized size measured on the
Raspberry Pi. Each setting was repeated 20 times; results report the
mean with $\pm1$ standard-deviation error bars unless otherwise stated.

For aggregate recovery, $Count_{\max}=N$ and
$Sum_{\max}=NV_{\max}$. With $N\leq2\times10^4$ and
$V_{\max}=100$, $Sum_{\max}\leq2\times10^6$, giving bounded BSGS
complexity $O(\sqrt{\max(N,NV_{\max})})$ and approximately
$1.4\times10^3$ baby-step entries at the maximum workload.
Energy consumption is excluded because calibrated power
instrumentation was unavailable; no model-derived estimates are
reported.

