\subsubsection{\textbf{Query Throughput}}

This experiment evaluates query-processing scalability with
$N=10{,}000$ records as the workload increases from 100 to
10,000 queries. The processing scope is identical to that of
Experiment~2, including all required ABSE $\mathsf{Test}$
operations, authenticated bitmap reconstruction, and conjunctive
bitmap evaluation. Aggregation, client-side verification, network
communication, and blockchain access are excluded.

For each workload of $N_Q$ queries, throughput is measured directly
from the wall-clock batch completion time $T_{\mathrm{batch}}$ as
\begin{equation}
\mathsf{Throughput}
=
\frac{N_Q}{T_{\mathrm{batch}}}.
\label{eq:query-throughput}
\end{equation}
Thus, the reported throughput represents sustained workload
processing rather than the reciprocal of isolated per-query latency.
The latter measures individual query-processing time, whereas batch
throughput additionally reflects runtime scheduling, resource
contention, and memory-access overhead as the workload increases.
Each workload is repeated over 5 independent runs -- fewer than the
20 used elsewhere in this evaluation, because a full query cycle
(fresh trapdoor plus search) is repeated up to 50 times per run for
the largest workload point, and Trinity~\cite{ref26}'s per-query cost
makes 20 repetitions impractical at this workload scale.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]
{fig_query_throughput_matched_colors.png}
\caption{Query throughput under increasing query workload.
Results show the mean over 5 independent runs; error bars
indicate $\pm1$ standard deviation.}
\label{fig:query_throughput}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:query_throughput} compares BVCRSA with
Trinity~\cite{ref26}, ABSE-Range~\cite{ref27},
Latt-IBEKS~\cite{ref28}, and VC-KASE~\cite{ref16}.
Throughput decreases with increasing workload because concurrent
query execution introduces greater cryptographic-processing,
scheduling, and resource-contention overhead. BVCRSA consistently
achieves the highest throughput owing to its hierarchical range-cover
organization and efficient word-parallel bitmap evaluation after
ABSE matching. Trinity~\cite{ref26} and ABSE-Range~\cite{ref27}
incur greater repeated search and index-evaluation costs, whereas
Latt-IBEKS~\cite{ref28} and VC-KASE~\cite{ref16} are dominated by
more expensive lattice- and pairing-based operations, respectively.
Overall, the results demonstrate that BVCRSA sustains higher
query-processing throughput as the workload increases.

\paragraph*{Discussion on Large-Scale Scalability.}
Although the prototype evaluation is limited to $N=10^5$ records,
Table~IV provides an analytical basis for larger IIoT workloads.
BVCRSA index construction requires
\[
O\!\left(Nm_p\log n_b+N_un_b+N\log N\right),
\]
whereas online query processing requires
\[
O\!\left(m_cN_u+|\mathcal U_Q|N/w+r\right).
\]
Accordingly, scaling to $N=10^6$--$10^7$ records yields
near-linear to $N\log N$ growth in the record-dependent terms when
the remaining parameters are fixed. This analysis indicates that
BVCRSA avoids quadratic growth in $N$ but should not be interpreted
as measured performance at million-record scale, since memory,
caching, concurrency, and distributed-system effects are not captured
by the asymptotic model. Empirical evaluation at
$N=10^6$--$10^7$ is therefore left for future work.

\subsubsection{\textbf{Verification Overhead}}

This experiment evaluates complete client-side verification
as the number of returned results varies from 50 to 500,
with the database size and query parameters fixed. For
BVCRSA, verification includes processing all
$r=|S_Q|$ aggregation entries $\{A_i\}_{pos_i\in S_Q}$,
checking their positions against $S_Q$, verifying the
aggregation Merkle multi-proof, and independently
recomputing $CT_{\mathrm{sum}}$ and $CT_{\mathrm{count}}$.
BVCRSA is compared with Trinity~\cite{ref26} and
VC-KASE~\cite{ref16}, which also support verifiable query
processing.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{fig_verification_overhead.png}
\caption{Verification time versus number of returned results.
Results show the mean over 20 independent runs; error bars
indicate $\pm1$ standard deviation.}
\label{fig:verification_overhead}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:verification_overhead} shows that BVCRSA
verification latency increases approximately linearly from
$0.35$~ms to $3.0$~ms as $|R_Q|$ increases. This growth
reflects verification of the returned aggregation entries
and Merkle multi-proof together with homomorphic ciphertext
recomputation; blockchain-state verification adds only
constant overhead. Trinity~\cite{ref26} increases from
approximately $1.4$~ms to $13.6$~ms due to per-result
authentication, whereas VC-KASE~\cite{ref16} remains near
$1.8$~ms because its verification is dominated by a fixed
number of pairing operations. BVCRSA therefore achieves the
lowest verification latency for $|R_Q|\lesssim250$ and,
although it exceeds VC-KASE for larger result sets, remains
substantially faster than Trinity while additionally
supporting verifiable encrypted aggregation and
blockchain-assisted query-state verification.

\subsubsection{\textbf{End-to-End Communication Overhead}}

This experiment measures the serialized communication volume
of the complete two-round BVCRSA query protocol. The reported
size includes the query tokens and selected positions, all
authenticated protected bitmap blocks returned for the matched
canonical nodes, all $r=|S_Q|$ authenticated aggregation
entries, their Merkle multi-proofs, and the two aggregate
ciphertexts. Blockchain synchronization traffic and the
encrypted records themselves are excluded because they are
not transmitted as part of the query response.

We vary the query selectivity from 10\% to 80\% and measure
the total request and response sizes in KB. The communication
cost increases with both the number of matched canonical nodes
and the result-set size: authenticated bitmap blocks dominate
the first response, whereas aggregation entries and their
authentication proofs increase with $r$. These measurements
confirm the analytical communication cost in
Table~\ref{tab:communication_cost} while explicitly accounting
for all objects transmitted by the complete verifiable query
protocol.




\subsubsection{\textbf{Effect of Homomorphic Aggregation}}

This experiment evaluates the scalability benefit of the
proposed bitmap-constrained homomorphic aggregation. The
number of matched records varies from 100 to 500 while the
query configuration remains fixed. BVCRSA is compared with
a Naive baseline that returns all matched encrypted records
to the user without in-network aggregation.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{fig_ablation_aggregation.png}
\caption{Effect of homomorphic aggregation versus naive record
retrieval. Results show the mean over 20 independent runs;
error bars indicate $\pm1$ standard deviation.}
\label{fig:ablation_aggregation}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:ablation_aggregation} compares the query
execution time as the number of matched records increases.
The Naive approach exhibits an almost linear increase,
rising from approximately 180~ms to nearly 900~ms because
every matched ciphertext must be transmitted and processed
individually by the user. In contrast, BVCRSA maintains
execution times below 20~ms across all workloads, with only
a modest increase as the number of matched records grows.
This improvement is achieved by performing Threshold
EC-ElGamal aggregation directly over the authenticated
aggregation entries selected by the bitmap filter, requiring
only compact aggregate ciphertexts and verification proofs
to be returned. Consequently, BVCRSA significantly reduces
communication and client-side processing overhead while
preserving verifiable encrypted aggregation, making it well
suited for large-scale IIoT analytics.

\subsubsection{\textbf{Aggregation Strategy Comparison}}

Aggregation latency is measured independently after query-result
selection and is therefore excluded from the query-processing
latencies reported in the preceding experiments.

This experiment compares BVCRSA's aggregate-then-decrypt strategy
with conventional decrypt-then-aggregate using the same
$(t,n)=(3,5)$ Threshold EC-ElGamal implementation over NIST P-256
specified in Phase~5. The matched result size varies from
$|R_Q|=10$ to $10{,}000$ for $N=10{,}000$. Results are reported
as the mean over 20 independent runs.

The conventional strategy threshold-decrypts every matched
ciphertext before aggregation. In contrast, BVCRSA first combines
the matched ciphertexts homomorphically and performs only two
threshold decryptions, one each for the resulting \emph{SUM} and
\emph{COUNT} ciphertexts. Each threshold decryption includes the
required partial-decryption shares and their combination.

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\textwidth]{fig_agg_strategy_comparison.png}
\caption{Aggregation strategy comparison:
(a) number of threshold decryptions,
(b) aggregation latency (log scale), and
(c) aggregation latency for small result sets.
Latency results in (b)--(c) are means over 20 independent runs;
error bars indicate $\pm1$ standard deviation.}
\label{fig:agg_strategy_comparison}
\end{figure*}

Figure~\ref{fig:agg_strategy_comparison}(a) shows that the
conventional strategy requires $|R_Q|$ threshold decryptions,
whereas BVCRSA requires only two, independent of the result size.
Accordingly, Fig.~\ref{fig:agg_strategy_comparison}(b) shows much
slower latency growth for BVCRSA, yielding up to a $670.5\times$
reduction at the largest workload. This trend is consistent with
the cost derivation in Table~IV: BVCRSA performs $O(r)$ lightweight
EC-point additions followed by a constant number of threshold
decryptions, whereas decrypt-then-aggregate incurs threshold
decryption for every selected ciphertext.

Figure~\ref{fig:agg_strategy_comparison}(c) further shows that
decrypt-then-aggregate is competitive only for very small result
sets, with a crossover near $|R_Q|\approx2$. Beyond this point,
repeated threshold decryptions dominate its cost. These results
therefore validate the derived aggregation-cost trend and demonstrate
the scalability advantage of aggregate-then-decrypt.


\subsubsection{\textbf{Aggregate-Recovery Scalability}}

This experiment evaluates bounded Baby-Step Giant-Step
(BSGS) recovery following Threshold EC-ElGamal decryption
using the same $(t,n)=(3,5)$ configuration over NIST P-256
specified in Phase~5. After combining the required partial
decryption shares, BSGS recovers the aggregate plaintext from
its EC-point representation. The maximum aggregate value
$M_{\max}$ varies from $10^3$ to $10^7$, with conventional
linear search used as the baseline. 

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{bsgs_scalability.png}
\caption{Aggregate-recovery latency versus maximum supported
aggregate value. Results show the mean over 20 independent
runs; error bars indicate $\pm1$ standard deviation.}
\label{fig:bsgs}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:bsgs} shows that BSGS latency grows gradually
