\section{Discussion}

BVCRSA is designed primarily for append-dominant IIoT workloads.
Dynamic updates affect only the corresponding range-cover nodes,
bitmap commitments, and Merkle paths, although very high update
rates may still increase maintenance overhead.

Only compact epoch commitments are anchored on-chain; searchable
indexes and encrypted records remain off-chain. Blockchain storage
therefore grows with the number of finalized epochs rather than
dataset size, although short epoch intervals may increase long-term
storage and synchronization costs.

The hierarchical architecture offloads intensive indexing, search,
and verification from resource-constrained sensors to the Edge
Gateway and cloud. This reduces sensor-side overhead but may make
the gateway a bottleneck at large scale. Physical tampering,
denial-of-service, and side-channel attacks remain outside the
present scope and require complementary system-level protections.

BVCRSA verifies query results by binding searchable indexes, bitmap
commitments, aggregation entries, and finalized epoch states.
However, it does not provide general verifiable computation for
bitmap evaluation or homomorphic aggregation; stronger guarantees
would require mechanisms such as zero-knowledge proofs, verifiable
computation, or trusted execution environments, with additional
overhead.

Finally, BVCRSA provides confidentiality subject to the formal
leakage profile. The cloud may observe canonical-node matches,
query repetition, selected positions, result cardinality, and
associated access patterns, while plaintext records and cryptographic
keys remain protected. Reducing such leakage through ORAM, PIR, or
other oblivious-access mechanisms is left for future work.
\section{Conclusion}

This paper presented BVCRSA, a blockchain-based
framework for verifiable conjunctive range search and
aggregation over encrypted IIoT data. BVCRSA integrates
attribute-based searchable encryption, authenticated
hierarchical range-cover indexing, protected bitmap
filtering, Threshold EC-ElGamal homomorphic aggregation,
and blockchain-assisted query-state verification into a
unified architecture. Unlike existing searchable
encryption schemes that primarily support secure
retrieval, BVCRSA jointly enables fine-grained access
control, conjunctive multi-range search,
privacy-preserving aggregation, threshold aggregate
decryption, and authenticated query-state verification.
Experimental results demonstrate efficient trapdoor
generation, practical bitmap-based query processing, low
verification overhead, and scalable index construction,
highlighting the practicality of BVCRSA for secure
outsourced IIoT analytics.Future work will investigate privacy-preserving indexed ABSE lookup
to avoid exhaustive token evaluation, forward-secure revocation with
epoch-evolving node keys, and compressed or query-scoped authenticated
bitmaps to reduce storage, communication, and authorized-user leakage.
Further directions include efficient dynamic index updates, distributed
trust management, large-scale real-world IIoT deployments, and
post-quantum primitives for long-term security.

